re ( Luo et al., 2024 ) and takes in as input aggregated features from the LDM UNet denoiser and a σ y \sigma_{y} embedding. It then outputs a log-variance map in latent space, parametrized as diagonal both in spatial and DCT domain, with the latter inspired by our theoretical results. We test richer parameterizations of the covariance in Appendix F.2 . We denote ANaLOG and ANaLOG -DCT as our method with the spatial and DCT covariances respectively. The exact architecture and training details are in Appendix E . 5.2 Main Results Tables 2 and 3 show that, for both datasets, ANaLOG consistently improves perceptual metrics over the native-latent baseline, SILO, on most tasks while maintaining a nearly identical inference time. This highlights our main conceptual message: modeling covariance structure is critical for native-latent guidance. Modeling the covariance in the DCT domain rather than in spatial domain yields further improvements in most tasks, especially for JPEG decompression, where the forward operator also operates in the DCT domain ( Wallace, 1991 ) . Compared with decode-and-guide baselines, ANaLOG generally provides a better quality-efficiency tradeoff, matching or exceeding these baselines on the FFHQ dataset. Notably, ANaLOG is 18 × 18	imes faster than STSL. On ImageNet, ANaLOG also yields substantial gains over SILO (e.g., 62.5 → 26.9 62.5	o 26.9 FID on Gaussian deblurring, 132.1 → 71.1 132.1	o 71.1 on 4× super-resolution), confirming that covariance modeling transfers beyond face datasets. A gap to decode-and-guide baselines remains on super-resolution; since both native-latent methods show degraded PSNR on ImageNet relative to FFHQ, we attribute this to difficulty of mean prediction rather than covariance. Improving μ θ \mu_{	heta} (e.g., by incorporating class labels as conditional inputs) is orthogonal to our contribution. Finally, results on inpainting and JPEG decompression (Table 3 ) suggest that, even when the exact sufficiency argument from Section 4 no longer applies, native-latent guidance remains empirically effective and continues to benefit from covariance-aware weighting. Figure 4 illustrates the qualitative differences among methods. The isotropic covariance in SILO leads to over-smoothed reconstructions that lack high-frequency detail. STSL recovers finer detail but can also introduce some visual artifacts. In contrast, ANaLOG produces visually plausible and consistent reconstructions. Additional qualitative results are provided in Appendix F.1 . Figure 4: Qualitative comparison on FFHQ deconvolution tasks. Best viewed zoomed in. Figure 5a visualizes the learned log-variance spatial maps for a single input across four tasks at two different timesteps. It highlights that the learned covariances are anisotropic, input- and time-dependent. Across different tasks, the variance captures operator-specific structure; e.g., in super-resolution it concentrates along edges and fine textures, where high-frequency information is lost. At early timesteps, the variance is relatively uniform, whereas at later timesteps, spatial structure emerges and becomes more pronounced. The learned covariance is also well-calibrated on held-out data across all tasks as shown in Appendix F.3 . (a) Learned spatial variance maps. Variance is averaged across the 4 latent channels. Variant FID ↓ \downarrow LPIPS ↓ \downarrow PSNR ↑ \uparrow Isotropic (SILO) 47.3 0.367 (.002) 25.03 (.06) Time-independent 41.09 0.378 (.002) 25.48 (.09) Theory-predicted 48.87 0.353 (.001) 25.86 (.05) ANaLOG 29.79 0.326 (.002) 27.34 (.09) (b) Ablation of covariance structure on Gaussian deblurring (FFHQ). Figure 5: Learned variance maps and ablation of covariance structure. (a) Task-dependent variance maps show the model learns distinct spatial patterns for each inverse problem. (b) Progressively richer covariance models improve reconstruction quality. Gaussian Blur 4x Super-Resolution 8x Super-Resolution Method Time (s) FID ↓ \downarrow LPIPS ↓ \d